{
  "abstract": "A string rewriting system is a finite set of rules l -> r between words. Its derivation graph has the words as vertices and the rewriting steps x l y -> x r y as edges. This entry proves in Lean 4 and Mathlib Squier's theorem: for a terminating system, one resolution cell per critical pair generates every homotopy between parallel zig-zag paths of derivations. Hence a finite convergent system over a finite alphabet has finite derivation type (Squier, Otto and Kobayashi 1994). Nine statements are compared.\nHomotopy modulo a set B of 2-cells (pairs of parallel paths) is the smallest relation on parallel paths that contains B and the independence squares (two steps rewriting disjoint factors commute), identifies a step followed by its inverse, in either order, with the empty path, is an equivalence relation, and is compatible with concatenation, inversion and whiskering by contexts x _ y. B is a homotopy basis if any two parallel paths are homotopic modulo B. A system has finite derivation type if it has a finite homotopy basis. A critical branching is a pair of distinct, non-independent steps from a common word with minimal context, that is, an overlap or an inclusion of two left-hand sides.\n(1) Newman's lemma for an arbitrary relation: terminating and locally confluent implies confluent. (2) The critical pair lemma for string rewriting: a system is locally confluent if and only if every critical branching is joinable. (3) Hence a terminating system is confluent if and only if its critical branchings are joinable. (4) Over a finite alphabet, a finite system has finitely many critical branchings. (5) Squier's theorem in homotopy-basis form: for a terminating system, every set of cells containing, for each critical branching, a cell formed by the branching and some resolution of it (in either orientation) is a homotopy basis. The resolutions are arbitrary, so every choice of one resolution per critical pair gives a homotopy basis. (6) Squier's theorem: a finite convergent system over a finite alphabet has finite derivation type. (7) Non-degeneracy: the homotopy relation does not identify everything. For the system aa -> e, bb -> e, bab -> aba and no cells, the two one-step rewrites aaa -> a are not homotopic, which a parity-weighted count of aa-steps detects. (8)-(9) That system, a complete presentation of the symmetric group S3 = <a, b | a^2 = b^2 = 1, bab = aba>, is terminating and confluent, and its five explicit critical-pair cells form a homotopy basis.\nThe proof of (5) follows Squier and Guiraud–Malbos. Normal-form paths are chosen by well-founded recursion. A noetherian induction then shows that every step followed by the normal-form path of its target is homotopic to the normal-form path of its source, resolving each local branching as an independence square, a trivial branching, or a whiskered critical cell. All results are classical. We searched for a previous machine-checked proof of Squier's theorem (November 2026) and found none; priority among formalizations is nevertheless unknown.",
